\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on three English IE benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} contains 551 test instances annotated for joint NER (6 types) and RE (7 types).
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} provides 3{,}453 test sentences with 4 entity types, where models achieve ${\approx}$91\% micro F1.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} consists of 1{,}287 social media instances with emerging entities, used to probe method boundaries (52\% zero F1).

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
Both \textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are fine-tuned with LoRA (rank$=$32, $\alpha{=}64$) for 5 epochs using LLaMA Factory \cite{zheng2024llamafactory} under identical training procedures, ensuring comparisons across the two model families (both at 8B scale) reflect model differences rather than training confounds.
We note a training confound for SciERC: Qwen3-8B's SciERC checkpoint was trained with \texttt{enable\_thinking=True} (LLaMA Factory 0.9.4 default), which prepends empty \texttt{<think>\textbackslash n</think>} blocks (4--5 tokens) to each training response and includes them in the loss computation; CoNLL-2003 and WNUT-17 checkpoints explicitly set \texttt{enable\_thinking=False}.
No reasoning content is present (the blocks are always empty), and constrained decoding at inference time enforces JSON-only output, so thinking tokens do not appear in generated samples.
This constitutes a minor train--test distribution mismatch for SciERC ($<$0.3\% of the 2048-token training window); CoNLL-2003 and WNUT-17 are unaffected.
We discuss implications in the Limitations section.
We draw $N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary; single seed, seed${=}42$), with $N{=}16$ for scaling experiments.\footnote{$N{=}8$ establishes the primary baseline at minimal compute cost; $N{=}16$ with multiple seeds verifies scaling behavior. $N$-scaling analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}) shows sublinear gains beyond $N{=}8$. $N{=}16$ results in Table~\ref{tab:robustness} (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}); 3-seed stability ($\sigma{\leq}0.026$; Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}, Appendix~\ref{app:multiseed}) confirms identical signal rankings across seeds.}
Quality estimation uses Spearman $\rho$ and AUROC; downstream utility uses best-of-$N$ selection macro F1 (instance-averaged) with exact span matching (NER) and head-tail-label matching (RE).
Inference uses vLLM with constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}
\label{sec:qe_results}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Quality estimation across tasks and models (Spearman $\rho$ and AUROC; $N{=}8$, seed${=}42$). Best value per column in \textbf{bold}. SciERC evaluates joint NER (6 types) and RE (7 types, single benchmark---preliminary); CoNLL evaluates NER (4 types). $N{=}16$ results appear in Table~\ref{tab:robustness} (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}).}
\label{tab:quality_estimation}
\begin{tabular}{ll cc cc cc cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC NER}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC RE}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (Qwen)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (LLaMA)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6} \cmidrule(lr){7-8} \cmidrule(lr){9-10}
Signal & Family & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC \\
\midrule
VC & Structural & \textbf{.379} & \textbf{.663} & \textbf{.350} & .694 & .424 & .789 & .426 & .709 \\
SJ  & Structural & .360 & .650 & .250 & .670 & .436 & .801 & \textbf{.431} & .704 \\
FK & Surface & .267 & .602 & .275 & .609 & .400 & .774 & .428 & .703 \\
EM  & Surface    & .295 & .614 & .134 & \textbf{.706} & \textbf{.451} & \textbf{.816} & \textbf{.431} & .705 \\
\midrule
LP     & Model      & .205 & .578 & .266 & .601 & .316 & .753 & .307 & \textbf{.761} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_signal_heatmap}
\caption{Quality estimation heatmap (Spearman $\rho$) across five signals and four configurations. Left: full evaluation set; right: conditional set (non-zero F1 instances). On the full set, structural signals dominate on SciERC NER while all signals converge on CoNLL. On the conditional set, EM emerges as the strongest signal on both NER ($\rho{=}0.360$) and RE ($\rho{=}0.417$), while LP RE collapses to near-zero ($\rho{=}0.011$).}
\label{fig:signal_heatmap}
\end{figure*}

Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} presents rank correlation ($\rho$) and AUROC for five confidence signals across tasks and models.

\paragraph{Difficulty-dependent signal complementarity.}
On SciERC NER (6 types), structural signals lead: VC $\rho{=}0.379$, SJ $\rho{=}0.360$, both outperforming surface ($\rho{\leq}0.295$) and LP ($\rho{=}0.205$); this advantage persists on RE (VC $\rho{=}0.350$; preliminary).
The best NER AUROC reaches 0.816 (EM, CoNLL Qwen), indicating strong binary discriminative power; pairwise bootstrap CIs confirm the SJ--LP gap ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.155$, CI excludes zero) but the SJ--EM gap is marginal (Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}).

\paragraph{$\rho$--AUROC dissociation.}
Signal choice depends on use case: on CoNLL, EM leads AUROC (0.816) despite a middling $\rho$ (0.451), preferred for binary triage; this dissociation replicates across configurations (LP leads AUROC at 0.761 on CoNLL LLaMA yet trails in $\rho$).

\paragraph{Signal rankings shift with task difficulty.}
On CoNLL (greedy F1${\approx}$0.91), EM leads ($\rho{=}0.451$, AUROC${=}0.816$); this crossover---EM${>}$SJ on easy tasks vs.\ SJ${>}$EM on hard tasks---demonstrates difficulty-dependent complementarity, consistent across model families.
On WNUT-17 (52\% zero F1), full-set VC achieves $\rho{=}0.724$ but conditional $\rho{=}0.038$ (n.s.; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}); LP exhibits strong \textit{negative} correlation ($\rho{=}{-}0.410$); all conditional signals collapse, suggesting a capability boundary beyond which consistency captures binary success/failure but not quality gradation.

\paragraph{Conditional filtering.}
Restricting to non-zero F1 instances reshuffles signal rankings (Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap}, right): EM emerges as the strongest conditional signal on both NER ($\rho{=}0.360$) and RE ($\rho{=}0.417$; preliminary), while structural signals drop substantially (VC NER: $0.379{\to}0.300$) and LP RE collapses to $\rho{=}0.011$ (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{The Correlation-Selection Gap}
\label{sec:selection_gap}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_selection_gap}
\caption{The correlation-selection gap on SciERC NER ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, 2-seed mean). Bars show best-of-$N$ selection F1; the line marks quality estimation $\rho$. Despite $\rho$ up to 0.40, no signal significantly outperforms greedy (all $p{>}0.05$; Table~\ref{tab:selection}). Dashed line indicates oracle upper bound. RE selection results in Table~\ref{tab:selection}.}
\label{fig:selection_gap}
\end{figure*}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ selection F1 on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). NER: 2-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$ ($n{=}551$), Fisher combined $p$ vs.\ greedy; RE: single seed (preliminary). Best non-oracle in \textbf{bold}. $^\dagger$Greedy: a fixed $T{=}1.0$ sample (not $T{=}0$), serving as the no-selection reference. VC selection uses majority-threshold voting ($\text{freq}{>}N/2$; see \S\ref{sec:signals}).}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & $p$ & RE F1 \\
\midrule
Oracle & $0.778 \pm 0.003$ & --- & 0.585 \\
\midrule
LP           & $\mathbf{0.647} \pm 0.005$ & .29 & 0.395 \\
\midrule
SJ           & $0.646 \pm 0.002$ & .68 & 0.386 \\
FK           & $0.646 \pm 0.001$ & .79 & --- \\
EM           & $0.637 \pm 0.001$ & .45 & 0.372 \\
VC           & $0.623 \pm 0.004$ & .26 & 0.359 \\
\midrule
Greedy$^\dagger$ & $0.645 \pm 0.001$ & --- & \textbf{0.404} \\
Random           & $0.610 \pm 0.003$ & --- & 0.354 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Despite strong quality estimation, confidence signals yield limited gains when used for best-of-$N$ selection (Table~\ref{tab:selection}, Figure~\ref{fig:selection_gap}).
Four of five signals are instance-level aggregates that assign a single score per input regardless of which sample is evaluated; they cannot rank candidates by construction.
The non-trivial question is whether log-probability---the only per-sample signal---can exploit the oracle headroom.
We anchor this analysis on SciERC, where output diversity is substantial (39--43\% unique outputs per instance, F1 std 0.11--0.14; Appendix~\ref{app:diversity}).

\paragraph{No significant improvement over greedy.}
We evaluate significance via 2-seed instance-level sign tests ($n{=}551$, Fisher combination).
On NER, the oracle (0.778) reveals 13.3~pp of headroom above greedy (0.645).
LP achieves the highest mean selection F1 (0.647), indistinguishable from greedy ($\Delta{=}{+}0.2$~pp, $p{=}0.29$); all other signals also fail to reach significance (all $p{>}0.05$; Table~\ref{tab:selection}), with 75--80\% of instances producing identical F1 under signal selection and greedy.
On RE, the best signal (LP, 0.395) falls below greedy (0.404, $-0.9$\,pp; single seed, preliminary), while 18.1~pp of oracle headroom remains.
VC, the best quality estimator ($\rho{=}0.379$), selects to only 0.623 on NER ($\Delta{=}{-}2.2$~pp, $p{=}0.26$); the direction is consistent with CoNLL and WNUT-17 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).

\paragraph{Seed variability.}
Individual seeds exhibit notable variance---even the most favorable seed falls short of significance ($p{=}0.19$)---motivating our multi-seed design.

\paragraph{Quality estimation $\neq$ output selection.}
Instance-level confidence signals predict \textit{which inputs} the model handles reliably, not \textit{which output} among $N$ candidates is best.
When per-sample variance is low relative to instance-level difficulty, even perfect quality estimation cannot discriminate among near-identical candidates.
This gap has direct consequences for test-time scaling in IE \cite{brown2024monkeys, snell2025scaling}: closing it likely requires per-sample signals such as trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers}.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}
\label{sec:robustness_main}

Consistency-based signals are robust across $T{\in}\{0.7, 1.0\}$ and show sublinear gains from $N{=}8$ to $N{=}16$ (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}, Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}); LP was excluded from the temperature comparison due to missing $T{=}0.7$ data (see Limitations).
NER signals are reasonably calibrated (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}); RE miscalibration (ECE up to 0.339, preliminary) imposes a ceiling at $\rho{\approx}0.28$ for $N{=}16$.

\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}\label{sec:formulation}

Given a language model $\mathcal{M}$ and input $x$, we sample $N$ structured outputs $\{y_1, \ldots, y_N\}$ at temperature $T > 0$ via constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}.
Each $y_i$ is an entity set (NER) or relation set (RE).
A confidence signal $c$ serves two purposes: (a) \textit{quality estimation}---predicting which instances the model handles reliably, and (b) \textit{sample selection}---identifying the best output among $N$ candidates.

A \textbf{per-sample signal} scores each $y_i$ independently, $c_{\text{ps}}: (x, y_i) \to \mathbb{R}$, enabling direct candidate ranking.
An \textbf{instance-level signal} aggregates over all $N$ samples, $c_{\text{il}}: (x, \{y_i\}_{i=1}^N) \to \mathbb{R}$, producing a single score per input.
This asymmetry---between predicting \textit{which inputs} are easy and selecting \textit{which output} is best---is central to the correlation-selection gap we investigate.
Notation is summarized in Appendix~\ref{app:notation}.

\subsection{Confidence Signals}\label{sec:signals}

We use \textit{confidence signal} as the umbrella term for all five measures.
Four are \textit{consistency signals}---computed from inter-sample agreement---spanning two families: \textit{structural} (Soft Jaccard [SJ], entity voting confidence [VC]) and \textit{surface} (exact match [EM], Fleiss' $\kappa$ [FK]).
The fifth, log-probability, is the sole \textit{model-internal} signal and the only one that scores individual samples rather than the instance as a whole.

\paragraph{Log-probability (model-internal, per-sample).}
The only per-sample signal.
For sample $y_i$ with tokens $t_1, \ldots, t_{|y_i|}$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:logprob}
c_{\text{lp}}(y_i) = \frac{1}{|y_i|} \sum_{j=1}^{|y_i|} \log p_{\mathcal{M}}(t_j \mid t_{<j}, x)
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Exact match voting (surface, instance-level).}
The majority fraction---the proportion of samples producing the most common output:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:em}
c_{\text{em}}(x) = \frac{1}{N} \max_{y} \big|\{i : y_i = y\}\big|
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Fleiss' $\kappa$ (surface, instance-level).}
We adapt \citet{fleiss1971agreement} to entity sets: items are unique (text, type) tuples across all $N$ samples; each sample assigns binary present/absent labels, yielding a $|E_\cup| \times 2$ rating matrix:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fk}
\kappa = \frac{\bar{P} - \bar{P}_e}{1 - \bar{P}_e}
\end{equation}
where $\bar{P}$ is mean observed pairwise agreement and $\bar{P}_e$ is expected agreement under random assignment.
While FK assumes independent raters, temperature-based sampling introduces mild dependence.
Empirically, excess agreement ($\bar{P} - \bar{P}_e$) is negative across all tested configurations ($-$0.4\% to $-$4.0\%, $n{=}5$ settings), indicating mild \textit{negative} dependence that does not inflate FK; this serves as approximate justification rather than a formal independence test.
Unlike structural signals, FK relies on exact string matching and does not tolerate surface variations.

\paragraph{Soft Jaccard (structural, instance-level).}
Our primary structural signal extends Jaccard similarity \cite{jaccard1901distribution} to extracted entity/relation sets.
For samples $y_i, y_j$, we group extractions by type, compute max-weight bipartite matching via the Hungarian algorithm (maximizing total token-level IoU; zero-IoU pairs automatically receive zero weight), and define:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sj}
\text{SJ}(y_i, y_j) = \frac{\sum_{m \in M} \text{sim}(a_m, b_m)}{\sum_{g} \max(|g_i|, |g_j|)}
\end{equation}
where $M$ is the matched set from the Hungarian algorithm (without minimum similarity threshold), $\text{sim}(a_m, b_m)$ is the token-level IoU (intersection of tokens divided by union of tokens) between matched entity spans $a_m$ and $b_m$, and $g_i, g_j$ index per-type extraction counts.
We use $\max(|g_i|, |g_j|)$ rather than $|g_i \cup g_j|$ as the normalizer to penalize length asymmetry between samples.
The instance-level signal averages over all pairs:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sj_instance}
c_{\text{sj}}(x) = \frac{1}{\binom{N}{2}} \sum_{i<j} \text{SJ}(y_i, y_j)
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Entity voting confidence (structural, instance-level).}
For each entity $e$ in the union $E_\cup$ of all samples, we compute frequency $\text{freq}(e) = |\{i : e \in y_i\}| / N$.
The instance-level confidence is the mean frequency:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:voting}
c_{\text{vote}}(x) = \frac{1}{|E_\cup|} \sum_{e \in E_\cup} \text{freq}(e)
\end{equation}
When $E_\cup = \emptyset$ (no entity extracted by any sample), we set $c_{\mathrm{vc}}(x) = 0$.\footnote{This occurs in $<$1\% of instances in our experiments (WNUT-17 only). Equation~\ref{eq:voting} defines the continuous QE signal used throughout Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} and all correlation analyses. For voted-output \textit{selection} (Table~\ref{tab:selection}), we include only entities with frequency ${>}N/2$, following standard majority voting. The two computations serve different purposes: Eq.~\ref{eq:voting} assigns a continuous quality score per instance; the majority threshold constructs a discrete output for F1 evaluation. On NER, the threshold variant yields $\rho{=}0.252$ (vs.\ $0.379$ for Eq.~\ref{eq:voting}), reflecting information loss from binarization; on RE, the difference is negligible ($\Delta\rho{=}0.019$).}

\subsection{Evaluation Framework}\label{sec:evaluation}

\paragraph{Quality estimation.}
We measure rank correlation (Spearman $\rho$) between the confidence signal and instance-level F1, and binary discrimination (AUROC) by classifying instances as above or below median F1.
Correlation analyses exclude instances with empty gold annotations ($n{=}529$ on SciERC after filtering 22/551), where F1 is trivially zero and consistency signals reflect agreement on the empty set rather than extraction quality; selection analyses retain the full set ($n{=}551$) because empty-gold instances contribute $\Delta{=}0$ to all signals, introducing no bias in the greedy-vs-signal comparison.

\paragraph{Sample selection.}
We report best-of-$N$ F1: for each instance, the signal selects one sample.
Per-sample signals (LP) directly rank candidates; instance-level signals select the sample closest to the consensus output (the consensus-proximity score for SJ is mathematically equivalent to leave-one-out SJ selection).
We compare against an \textit{oracle} (highest-F1 sample) and a \textit{greedy} baseline (a fixed sample from the $T{=}1.0$ candidate pool, providing a no-selection reference within the sampled distribution).

\paragraph{Complementarity.}
High rank correlation does not guarantee effective sample selection: if per-sample variance is low, even a perfect quality estimator cannot distinguish among near-identical candidates (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).

